Title:
IDAssist — AI-Powered Reverse Engineering Plugin for IDA Pro

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
- IDAssist is an IDA Pro 9.0+ plugin that embeds LLM-assisted reverse engineering directly into IDA via a dockable PySide6 UI.  
- It aims to speed up binary analysis by generating function explanations, answering interactive questions with context macros, and proposing automated renames for functions/variables/types that can be applied back into the IDB.  
- The tool builds a semantic knowledge graph of the binary (call relationships, data flows, security characteristics) with visualization, semantic search, and community detection to help map large codebases.  
- It also supports RAG by indexing uploaded reference documents (hybrid text+vector search using Whoosh) to ground LLM responses in user-provided material.  
- Multiple LLM backends are supported (OpenAI, Anthropic, Ollama, LiteLLM, AWS Bedrock), plus MCP/tool-calling and an autonomous ReAct agent mode for multi-step investigations.  
- Useful for reverse engineers, malware analysts, and red teamers doing rapid triage and comprehension of unfamiliar binaries; also relevant to defenders analyzing suspicious samples.

Technical Focus:
- IDA Pro plugin development (Python, PySide6, IDA API main-thread execution)
- LLM provider abstraction (OpenAI/Anthropic/Ollama/LiteLLM/AWS Bedrock)
- Tool/function calling + MCP integration (SSE/HTTP/stdio)
- ReAct agent workflows and “extended thinking”/reasoning budgets
- Semantic knowledge graph generation (call graph, xrefs, community detection)
- RAG document ingestion and hybrid search (Whoosh, local persistence via SQLite)

Use Cases:
- Rapid function-level understanding and security-relevant API/vuln pattern triage in decompiled code
- Automated rename/type suggestion workflows to clean up large IDBs
- Building and searching a binary-wide knowledge graph to identify modules and suspicious clusters
- Grounding analysis with internal docs, prior reports, or protocol specs via RAG
- Semi-autonomous multi-step investigation using ReAct + tool-calling to query xrefs, ranges, and symbols

Keywords:
IDA Pro, Hex-Rays Decompiler, IDA plugin, Python, PySide6, LLM, OpenAI, Anthropic, Ollama, LiteLLM, AWS Bedrock, Converse API, MCP, function calling, ReAct agent, semantic knowledge graph, call graph, cross-references, RAG, Whoosh, SQLite